<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2008">2008</a>–<a href="/years/2015">2015</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> was established in 2008 under the settlement of lawsuits by former pupils of Canada's Indian residential schools, church-run boarding schools to which Indigenous children were sent from the nineteenth century until the 1990s. After hearing thousands of survivors, it issued its final report in 2015, which described the system as cultural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and set out 94 calls to action.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission was set up on 1 June 2008 as one of the requirements of the Indian Residential Schools Settlement Agreement, reached between survivor groups, the Assembly of First Nations, church bodies and the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, with a budget of $60 million over five years. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s commission it was not a state-led body, and it had no power of subpoena, no power to offer amnesty and no power to name individuals. On 11 June 2008 Prime Minister Stephen Harper apologised for the role of past governments in running the schools, about 70 percent of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> had administered with government funds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its first chair, Justice Harry S. Laforme of the Ontario Court of Appeal, resigned on 20 October 2008, accusing the other commissioners, Claudette Dumont-Smith and Jane Brewin Morley, of insubordination; both left in January 2009. On 10 June 2009 Murray Sinclair became chair, with Marie Wilson and Wilton Littlechild as commissioners.</p><p>Over six years the commission travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and heard more than 6,500 witnesses in community hearings, sharing circles and panels, many of whom described being stripped of their language and culture and suffering abuse, sexual assault and malnutrition. Records supplied by Library and Archives Canada in 2014, after a court order of January 2013, led to an extension. The commission held its closing event in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> from 31 May to 3 June 2015 and in June released its summary and 94 calls to action, divided into Legacy and Reconciliation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The final report of December 2015 estimated that about 150,000 children attended the schools over their 120-year history and that about 3,200 died in them. Its records went to the National Centre for Truth and Reconciliation at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> in Winnipeg, which opened in November 2015 and holds more than five million documents. Critics such as Glen Coulthard argued that it treated colonialism as a closed chapter, while others, among them Hymie Rubenstein and Rodney A. Clifton, faulted its use of evidence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 2018, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in 2021, set up commissions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Saami</a> modelled on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>First Nations on the prairies lost most of their lands through the numbered treaties of 1871–1921, and many Indigenous children were sent to residential schools, which a <b>Truth and Reconciliation Commission</b> described in 2015 as cultural genocide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
